    "cost_closing_html":  "About \u003cstrong\u003e$1.81 a bowl\u003c/strong\u003e. The dried chiles look expensive on the shelf, but a bag lasts through several batches, so the real per bowl cost of the flavor base is small.",
    "shop_smart":  [
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eBuy dried chiles in the big bag from the Hispanic aisle.\u003c/strong\u003e The branded packets on the spice rack cost several times more per ounce for the exact same dried chile.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eCanned hominy saves you a day.\u003c/strong\u003e The dried version needs soaking and hours of cooking. Canned is rinse and go, and the per serving price is about the same.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003ePork shoulder is the correct cut and the cheap one.\u003c/strong\u003e This is one of those happy cases where the traditional choice is also the value choice. Bone-in makes better broth.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eToasting the chiles is a free upgrade.\u003c/strong\u003e Thirty seconds a side in a dry pan and the sauce goes from flat to deep. It costs nothing but attention."
                   ],
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot and write the number down for portioning later.",
                    "Stem and seed the dried guajillo and ancho chiles, toast them in a dry skillet about 30 seconds a side until they smell nutty, then soak them in hot water for 20 minutes.",
                    "Blend the softened chiles with some of their soaking water, several cloves of garlic, the chili powder, ground cumin, dried oregano and a spoon of cocoa powder into a smooth sauce, then strain it.",
                    "Cube the pork shoulder, season it with salt and black pepper, and brown it hard in the vegetable oil in your big pot, working in batches.",
                    "Fry the strained chile sauce in the pot for five minutes so it darkens and loses its raw edge, then return all the pork.",
                    "Pour in the chicken broth, add the dried arbol chiles whole if you want heat, and simmer covered for about 2 hours until the pork is fully tender.",
                    "Add the drained hominy and simmer 20 minutes more so it takes on the red broth, then cook the rice per the package to serve alongside."
                ],
